/* `hash.id(value)`'s own doc comment promises `hash.id(a) ==
 * hash.id(b)` iff `a`/`b` collide as dict keys; routing the
 * underlying 64-bit hash through a plain `number` broke that for
 * roughly half of all possible hash outputs (anything past 2^53
 * silently rounds through f64). It should promote to bigint instead,
 * exactly like struct's integer codes already do.
 *
 * FxHasher (what hash.id uses internally) has a fixed, unseeded
 * algorithm, so hashing the same fixed set of keys always produces
 * the same values on every run/machine; this is a real,
 * deterministic regression test, not a probabilistic one. */
import hash

var saw_bigint = false
var saw_number = false
iter var i = 0; i < 200; i += 1 {
  var h = hash.id('key_${i}')
  if typeof(h) == 'bigint' {
    saw_bigint = true
  } else {
    saw_number = true
  }
}
echo saw_bigint
echo saw_number

/* Same input always hashes to the same value, whichever type it
 * lands on; the promotion itself must not break determinism. */
echo hash.id('stable') == hash.id('stable')
